      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.087,
            0.452,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": "Ever heard of ..."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.125,
            0.636,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "In 1665, a scientist named Robert Hooke published a book called Micrographia. He was a careful observer, and a skilled artist. In this book, he showed detailed drawings of tiny things that people had never seen before—things he saw using a tool we now call a microscope."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.243,
            0.636,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "His microscope made things look 200 to 300 times bigger, than what one could see with the unaided eye. One day, he looked at a thin slice of cork and saw it was made of many small, empty spaces. These compartments reminded him of a honeycomb. He drew what he saw and called each small space a cell. This was the first time the word cell was used in science to describe the basic unit of life."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.412,
            0.636,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "Around the same time in 1660s, Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a Dutch scientist, made better lenses that allowed him to build more useful microscopes. He was the first person to clearly see and describe tiny living things like bacteria and blood cells. Because of this, he is known as the Father of Microbiology."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.678,
            0.128,
            0.826,
            0.144
          ],
          "content": "**MICROGRAPHIA:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.716,
            0.147,
            0.787,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "OR SOME"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.683,
            0.164,
            0.822,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "Physiological Descriptions OF"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.68,
            0.197,
            0.823,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "**MINUTE BODIES** MADE BY"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.649,
            0.234,
            0.853,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": "**MAGNIFYING GLASSES.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.729,
            0.252,
            0.775,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "WITH"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.644,
            0.266,
            0.858,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "OBSERVATIONS and INQUIRIES thereupon. By R. HOOKE, Fellow of the ROYAL SOCIETY Non poifis oculo quantum contendere Lincent, Non tamen idcirco contemnas Lippus inungi. Horat. Ep. lib. 1."
        },
